  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nWeb cam imagery, surface observations, spotter reports and radar\nimagery also show rain has been changing to snow over the past \nfew hours across Schoharie County, the Helderbergs and eastern\nCatskills. The rain is now also starting to change over to snow\nacross the Capital and Saratoga Regions and this slow transition\nfrom rain to snow will continue to spread east and southeast\nthrough the rest of the overnight hours. Most locations in the\nforecast area will see some wet flakes before the storm departs. \n\nSurface low pressure is now located just south of Long Island\nand it will continue to slowly track eastward south of southern\nNew England through the day today. Meanwhile, a large upper\nlevel trough, currently centered over the eastern Great Lakes,\nwill continue to build towards the area through the day as well.\nCAMs suggest steady precip on the northwest side of the storm\nsystem will continue across much of the area through the rest of\nthe overnight hours. After daybreak, precip will start to taper\noff from north to south as the storm starts to depart, although\na lingering surface trough will keep some light precip going to\nthe south. Most areas should be done with precip by the late\nmorning or early afternoon hours, although it will probably stay\nfairly cloudy through the day, with temps remaining below\nnormal. Highs will only be in the 30s, with some low 40s\npossible for the Hudson Valley.\n\nTotal snowfall will be highly variable across the region and \nwill depend on elevation. The highest totals look to be in \nSchoharie County and the eastern Catskills, especially above \n1500 feet, where a Winter Weather Advisory remains in effect. \nMost spots will see snow totals of 2 to 5 inches in the \nAdvisory area, although some localized totals in the highest \nterrain of 6-7 inches can't be ruled out, especially considering\nwhat has occurred upstream of the area over Otsego County in \ncentral NY. Some isolated power outages can't be ruled out where\nthe heaviest totals occur, as the heavy, wet nature of the \nsnowfall could down tree limbs or power lines, although this \nwill likely be fairly isolated, as the heaviest totals may wind \nup occurring over mainly rural forested areas. High terrain \nareas of the Adirondacks and southern Greens have been seeing \nsnowfall since early in the day on Thursday, and some additional\nlight accumulation will continue to occur in these areas \nthrough daybreak as well.\n\nEven valley areas (such as the Capital and Saratoga Regions and\nparts of the mid Hudson Valley) may see a light accumulation of\na dusting to an inch (perhaps up to 2 inches in a spots), but \nthis will mainly be on grassy and unpaved surfaces and it will \nstart to melt once precip ends. While travel issues aren't \nexpected in the Capital Region due to the warmer road surfaces, \nsome walkways, decks, or outdoor steps could be slippery, so use\ncaution if you'll be out early in the day. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nOver the weekend, the upper level trough will remain overhead.\nThis will keep temps below normal through the weekend, with\ndaytime temps only in the 30s and 40s and overnight lows in the\n20s to low 30s. Plenty of clouds will remain in place due to the\ncyclonic flow and some upslope/lake enhanced rain/snow showers\nwill be possible, mainly for northern and western areas. The\nbulk of this precip will be west or north of the region, but the\nsouthern Greens, western Adirondacks, western Mohawk Valley and\neastern Catskills may see some on and off light showers through\nthe weekend.  Any snow accumulation looks very minor.\n\nBehind this departing upper level trough, rising heights and\nwarming temps aloft are expected through the week. A large area\nof surface high pressure will build towards the area from the\nwest. This will allow for dry weather through the upcoming week\nwith temps gradually rising each day. Daytime temps will likely\nreturn to above normal levels by Tuesday or Wednesday and there\naren't any signs on any additional widespread precip events in\nthe model guidance through the upcoming week.",
